# Can we test whether markets work in a lab?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What did Vernon Smith's double auctions show? Why did prices converge with only a few traders?"
next: {"title":"What happened when markets were allowed in?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/66-what-happened-when-markets-were-allowed.cast"}

## A wind tunnel for markets
sources:
    - id: samnord85
      title: Economics (12th edition)
      authors: Paul A. Samuelson and William D. Nordhaus
      year: 1985
      finding: The textbook told students that economists, unlike chemists or biologists, cannot easily run controlled experiments (paraphrased; widely quoted, e.g. by John List).
    - id: nobel02
      title: 'Foundations of behavioral and experimental economics: Daniel Kahneman and Vernon Smith (advanced information)'
      authors: Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences
      year: 2002
      finding: 'Smith''s 1962 double-auction experiments: traders knowing only their own values traded close to the competitive price; Plott and Smith (1978) found posted prices converged more slowly; the lab as a ''wind tunnel'' for market designs.'
    - id: smith1962
      title: An Experimental Study of Competitive Market Behavior
      authors: Vernon L. Smith
      year: 1962
      finding: Double auctions begun at Purdue in 1956, with 'a practical number of marketers, say twenty, thirty, or forty', converged toward the competitive equilibrium when all bids, offers and trades were public.
    - id: coursey
      title: Vernon Smith (Econlib feature)
      authors: Don Coursey
      year: 2002
      finding: A market with as little as four sellers and four buyers will produce the competitive outcome in Smith-style double auctions.

