    card title "A wind tunnel for markets"

Can you test whether markets work the way engineers test  (@draw q1@)a wing, in a wind tunnel?
    draw wind plane parallel true

B: For a long time th (@draw book@)e textbooks said no. As late as 1985, Samuelson and Nordhaus's (@draw tb@) best-seller told students that economists can't run controlled experiments the way chemists can.

Yet by then a (@draw tb_x@) lab tradition was nearly thirty years old, a (@draw smith@)nd its founder, Vernon Smith, would share the 2002  (@draw nobel@)Nobel prize for it.

    erase plane wind q1 book tb tb_x smith nobel

His trick, in short: (@draw bcard scard parallel true@) hand buyers secret values and sellers secret costs, let them call out bids and (@draw dline sline dlab slab parallel true@) asks, and watch whether prices find where the hidden curves cross.  (@draw pstar pstarl@)Within a few rounds, they did.

    erase bcard scard dline sline dlab slab pstar pstarl

B: But textbook competition needs so many traders that  (@draw n_scale@)no one matters. Smith's markets had twenty to forty.

So later experimenters thinned the markets out, to see how few traders it takes before the price stops finding the crossing.

So how few traders could you get away with?
    ask "How many traders per side does a double auction need to reach the competitive price? Click the line." on n_scale store g default "20"
        right: About {g.true} of each: you said {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}. Smith's own markets had twenty to forty, but as few as {g.true} of each was enough.

B: As few as four of each was enough. That's thinner than most markets anyone worries about.
    draw four

    erase four n_scale

So we'll follow the lab three steps further (@draw m1@): why so few are enough, what (@draw m2@) happens when you change the (@draw m3@) rules, and when prices fly off altogether.

