    text pub "every bid public,\nno collusion" #2f6b8f cites ["smith1962"] x 270 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true
    text posted "posted: above $2,\nfewer trades" #b5482e cites ["plottsmith78"] x 270 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Why four are enough"

Four of each sounds far too few to compete. So where does the competition come from?
    draw axes

B: From the edge of the market. The price is pinned by t (@draw edge@)he traders just in and just out: the last buyer who gets a unit, and the next one, who would buy if it were a little cheaper.

    erase edge

Here's a computer version of the lab you can run: four buyers and four sellers, with values and costs that cross  (@draw line_2@)at two dollars. Illustrative numbers.
    draw sim sim_ctl_n sim_ctl_rules rd rr parallel true

Each dot is one t (@draw line_1@)rade, five trading periods side by side. The traders are crude: they open greedy, then edge their bids up or their asks down until someone takes them.

B: And between periods, whoever traded tries f (@highlight line_1@)or a better deal next time, and whoever was left out gets keener.

Prices land aroun (@highlight line_2 effect pulse@)d two dollars from the first period on.

B: Because every miss is public. A seller asking to (@highlight line_1 effect pulse@)o much watches a rival's lower ask take the sale, in front of everyone.

That was Smith's own summary: rule out collusion (@draw pub@), make every bid, offer and trade public, and the competitive price follows. Great numbers are not on the list.

    erase pub

Twelve a side looks much the same.
    run code sim values.n [4,8,12]

Even two (@run code sim values.n [2] smooth false@) a side stays close, though a market that thin is a fragile thing to bet on.

B: Now change only the rules. Selle (@run code sim values.n [4] values.rules ["posted prices"] smooth false@)rs post one price for the whole period, take it or leave it, like a shop.

Prices sit above two dollars, an (@draw posted@)d fewer units change hands, so gains are lost. Plott and Smith found it in 1978: posted prices came down more slowly, from above.

    erase posted

Try it: flip between the two rules and slide the traders from two to twelve. Which moves the dots more, the number of traders or the rules?
    explore code sim

