sources:
    - id: ssw88
      title: Bubbles, Crashes, and Endogenous Expectations in Experimental Spot Asset Markets
      authors: Vernon L. Smith, Gerry L. Suchanek and Arlington W. Williams
      year: 1988
      finding: 'Fourteen of twenty-two experiments showed price bubbles followed by crashes relative to the dividend value; the asset paid a random dividend after each of fifteen periods (classic design: 0, 8, 28 or 60 cents, equally likely, so a $3.60 starting value falling 24 cents a period); experience reduced but did not eliminate bubbles.'
    - id: dlm05
      title: 'Bubbles and Experience: An Experiment'
      authors: Martin Dufwenberg, Tobias Lindqvist and Evan Moore
      year: 2005
      finding: Even with a minority (a third) of experienced traders, bubbles are substantially abated.
    - id: hps08
      title: 'Thar She Blows: Can Bubbles Be Rekindled with Experienced Subjects?'
      authors: Reshmaan N. Hussam, David Porter and Vernon L. Smith
      year: 2008
      finding: Bubbles shrink with experience but can persist into a third session; a large rise in liquidity and dividend uncertainty rekindles them among experienced traders.

    scratch sc top-right cites ["ssw88"] work ["dividend: 0, 8, 28 or 60¢","average: 24¢ a period","× 15 periods = $3.60"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text big "14 of 22 markets" #b5482e cites ["ssw88"] x 760 y 600 font_size 44 hidden true
    text exptx "same traders again:\nsmaller bubble" #2f6b8f cites ["ssw88","hps08"] x 760 y 600 font_size 32 hidden true
    text third "a third experienced:\nmuch smaller" #2f6b8f cites ["dlm05"] x 760 y 600 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Bubbles in captivity"

So in a market for goods, the lab finds the price. Smith then asked about a market for an asset, where the price rests on what people expect.
    draw axes

B: In 1988 he built one with Gerry Suchanek and Arlington Williams. A share lives fifteen tra (@draw sc sc_line_1@)ding periods, and after each it pays a dividend: zero, eight, twenty-eight or sixty cents, all equally likely.

That's twent (@draw sc_line_2@)y-four cents a period on average, so at the start a share i (@draw sc_line_3@)s worth fifteen times that: three dollars sixty.

B: And each period one dividend is (@draw line_1@) gone, so the value falls by twenty-four cents, all the way down. The traders are told the odds and the number of periods.

    erase sc

    quiz "Traders who all know what a share is worth: what did prices do?" judge false voice b pool false
        right: Here's what they did.
        * Tracked the falling value
        * Stayed a little below it
        * Rose far above it, then crashed

A bubble  (@draw line_2@)and a crash, in fourteen of their twenty-two ma (@draw big@)rkets. Sketched, the typical shape: prices start below the value, climb far above it while the value falls, and crash near the end.

B: With an ass (@highlight line_2@)et whose value you could work out on a napkin.

    erase big

Then they brought the same traders ba (@animate stage 1 duration 3 ghost true@)ck. With each market they had already (@draw exptx@) played, the bubble shrank, though in some it survived even a third time.

    erase exptx

B: And you don't need everyone experienced. In 2005 Dufwenberg, Lindqvist and Moore foun (@draw third@)d that with only a third of traders who'd done it before, bubbles were much smaller.

    erase third

So the same lab that watched markets find th (@highlight line_1 line_2@)e price also grew bubbles in captivity. Same kind of double auction; only the thing traded had changed.

## Greed, confusion, or the lab?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Vernon Smith would hire you as a lab assistant.
    good: 'A solid run: the market cleared.'
    poor: A bubble in your answers; experience will shrink it.
    none: Even the lab needs traders who show up.
sources:
    - id: lnp01
      title: 'Nonspeculative Bubbles in Experimental Asset Markets: Lack of Common Knowledge of Rationality vs. Actual Irrationality'
      authors: Vivian Lei, Charles N. Noussair and Charles R. Plott
      year: 2001
      finding: In a setting where speculation is not possible, bubbles and crashes are still observed.
    - id: khs12
      title: 'Thar She Bursts: Reducing Confusion Reduces Bubbles'
      authors: Michael Kirchler, Jürgen Huber and Thomas Stöckl
      year: 2012
      finding: A declining fundamental value confused traders, who expected it to stay flat; framing the asset as shares in a depletable gold mine reduced mispricing.
    - id: hps08
      title: 'Thar She Blows: Can Bubbles Be Rekindled with Experienced Subjects?'
      authors: Reshmaan N. Hussam, David Porter and Vernon L. Smith
      year: 2008
      finding: A large increase in liquidity and dividend uncertainty rekindled bubbles among experienced traders.
    - id: list04
      title: Testing Neoclassical Competitive Theory in Multilateral Decentralized Markets
      authors: John A. List
      year: 2004
      finding: In field experiments with sportscard and collector-pin traders, exchange prices moved toward the competitive prediction within a few market periods.

