    card title "Greed, confusion, or the lab?"

B: But why  (@draw spec conf parallel true@)would anyone pay nearly three dollars for a share worth one and a half?

One answer is specu (@highlight spec@)lation: you overpay because you expect to sell to someone who'll overpay more.

B: So take away the chance to resell. Lei, Noussair and (@draw spec_e@) Plott did that in 2001, and bubbles formed anyway.

The other ans (@highlight conf@)wer is confusion. In 2012 Kirchler, Huber and Stöckl found traders expected the value to stay flat, not to fall.

B: Call the shar (@draw conf_e@)es a gold mine that runs out, and the bubbles shrank.

And experience is narrower than it looks. In 2008 Hussam, Porter and Smith gave (@draw cash@) experienced traders more cash and riskier dividends, and the bubble came back.

    erase spec conf spec_e conf_e cash

B: And is a room of stude (@draw cards@)nts trading for pocket money a real market at all?

John List took the test to sportscard dealers at trade shows  (@draw list@)in 2004, and their prices too moved toward the competitive prediction within a few periods.

    erase cards list

So how sure are we? That double auctions find (@draw r1 c1@) the competitive price, even with few traders: consensus. That the  (@draw r2 c2@)rules change how fast: mostly agreed.

B: That novices (@draw r3 c3@) blow bubbles in these asset markets: mostly agreed. Why, and  (@draw r4 c4@)what it says about real stock markets: contested.

    hide r1 c1 r2 c2 r3 c3 r4 c4

    show q1i

    quiz "Harbourtown's quay starts a fish auction: five boats, six restaurants, every bid called out loud. What does the lab predict?"
        intro: Three new cases. First, Harbourtown.
        right: Near the competitive price: in the lab, public bids and asks did the work, and as few as four of each was enough.
        wrong: In the lab, public bids did the work, and as few as four of each was enough: here, near the competitive price within a few days.
        * Prices far from competitive: too few traders
        + Near the competitive price within a few days
        * Hardly any trade at all

    hide q1i

    show q2i

    quiz "An online market switches from live bidding to sellers posting a fixed price for the week. Lab evidence says prices will…" voice b
        right: Sit above it and adjust more slowly: a posted price can only be fixed once a period, so mistakes linger.
        wrong: With a price fixed for the whole week, nobody can correct a mistake until the next one: here, it sits above and adjusts more slowly.
        * Fall below the competitive level
        + Sit above it and adjust more slowly
        * Not change at all

    hide q2i

    show q3i

    quiz "A new token trades among first-timers, and its value runs down over time. Which change would the lab expect to shrink a bubble most?"
        right: Add experienced traders: even a third of them shrank bubbles, while more cash brought bubbles back.
        wrong: Experienced traders shrank bubbles, while more cash brought them back: here, add traders who have been through such a market.
        * Pour in more cash
        + Add traders who have been through such a market
        * Hide how the value falls

    hide q3i

    show r1 c1 r2 c2 r3 c3 r4 c4

So can we test whether markets work in a lab? Yes, and the lab's answer is conditional: a marke (@highlight r1 r4@)t finds the price when every offer is public and the traders understand what they are trading.

