      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||So we''ll follow the lab three steps further: why so few are enough, what happens when you change the rules, and when prices fly off altogether.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1q9i13n'
    '|a||Four of each sounds far too few to compete. So where does the competition come from?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5080
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||From the edge of the market. The price is pinned by the traders just in and just out: the last buyer who gets a unit, and the next one, who would buy if it were a little cheaper.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11560
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:j6bcau'
    '|a||Here''s a computer version of the lab you can run: four buyers and four sellers, with values and costs that cross at two dollars. Illustrative numbers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1gyuav5'
    '|a||Each dot is one trade, five trading periods side by side. The traders are crude: they open greedy, then edge their bids up or their asks down until someone takes them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:xjdjss'
    '|b||And between periods, whoever traded tries for a better deal next time, and whoever was left out gets keener.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:p6fyxx'
    '|a||Prices land around two dollars from the first period on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:czpv8g'
    '|b||Because every miss is public. A seller asking too much watches a rival''s lower ask take the sale, in front of everyone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7560
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:you52k'
    '|a||That was Smith''s own summary: rule out collusion, make every bid, offer and trade public, and the competitive price follows. Great numbers are not on the list.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1yb9y4r'
    '|a||Twelve a side looks much the same.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:t7dy3t'
    '|a||Even two a side stays close, though a market that thin is a fragile thing to bet on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5760
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:tmddcf'
    '|b||Now change only the rules. Sellers post one price for the whole period, take it or leave it, like a shop.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:9qm102'
    '|a||Prices sit above two dollars, and fewer units change hands, so gains are lost. Plott and Smith found it in 1978: posted prices came down more slowly, from above.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:t5xdbk'
    '|a||Try it: flip between the two rules and slide the traders from two to twelve. Which moves the dots more, the number of traders or the rules?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8920
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1qggxdm'
    '|a||So in a market for goods, the lab finds the price. Smith then asked about a market for an asset, where the price rests on what people expect.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10400
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||In 1988 he built one with Gerry Suchanek and Arlington Williams. A share lives fifteen trading periods, and after each it pays a dividend: zero, eight, twenty-eight or sixty cents, all equally likely.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12600
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1uqzh4a'
    '|a||That''s twenty-four cents a period on average, so at the start a share is worth fifteen times that: three dollars sixty.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1tancji'
    '|b||And each period one dividend is gone, so the value falls by twenty-four cents, all the way down. The traders are told the odds and the number of periods.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1wj5cg8'
    '|b||Traders who all know what a share is worth: what did prices do?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4080
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1oav1sa'
    '|b||Here''s what they did.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1640
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||A bubble and a crash, in fourteen of their twenty-two markets. Sketched, the typical shape: prices start below the value, climb far above it while the value falls, and crash near the end.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13920
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||With an asset whose value you could work out on a napkin.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:ugbb1g'
    '|a||Then they brought the same traders back. With each market they had already played, the bubble shrank, though in some it survived even a third time.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:ydctfs'
    '|b||And you don''t need everyone experienced. In 2005 Dufwenberg, Lindqvist and Moore found that with only a third of traders who''d done it before, bubbles were much smaller.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1wzu5cs'
    '|a||So the same lab that watched markets find the price also grew bubbles in captivity. Same kind of double auction; only the thing traded had changed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:12qdk58'
    '|b||But why would anyone pay nearly three dollars for a share worth one and a half?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||One answer is speculation: you overpay because you expect to sell to someone who''ll overpay more.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7040
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:152ajsf'
    '|b||So take away the chance to resell. Lei, Noussair and Plott did that in 2001, and bubbles formed anyway.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7600
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:166a0ig'
    '|a||The other answer is confusion. In 2012 Kirchler, Huber and Stöckl found traders expected the value to stay flat, not to fall.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:7ttn60'
    '|b||Call the shares a gold mine that runs out, and the bubbles shrank.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4440
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1awwovi'
    '|a||And experience is narrower than it looks. In 2008 Hussam, Porter and Smith gave experienced traders more cash and riskier dividends, and the bubble came back.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:svbqmc'
    '|b||And is a room of students trading for pocket money a real market at all?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4480
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:nddc4a'
    '|a||John List took the test to sportscard dealers at trade shows in 2004, and their prices too moved toward the competitive prediction within a few periods.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1dyzbnm'
    '|a||So how sure are we? That double auctions find the competitive price, even with few traders: consensus. That the rules change how fast: mostly agreed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:jh5vcb'
    '|b||That novices blow bubbles in these asset markets: mostly agreed. Why, and what it says about real stock markets: contested.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8600
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1azjaxd'
    '|a||Three new cases. First, Harbourtown. Harbourtown''s quay starts a fish auction: five boats, six restaurants, every bid called out loud. What does the lab predict?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10960
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:2swjif'
    '|a||Near the competitive price: in the lab, public bids and asks did the work, and as few as four of each was enough.':
